SIX edits to public/index.html, all in place. The ids #back-link, #group-list and #group-title do not change, so every selector in .sdlc/memory/qa/selectors.md still resolves and the reserved memory file does not need editing. NO element moves: the back control stays at line 74, ahead of the heading at line 75 and #expense-desc at line 79. That order is what AC-3 and the qa script are written against, so changing it would invalidate both.
--- (1) STYLESHEET: insert immediately after the existing `button:hover { background: #1d4ed8; }` rule, still inside the <style> block ---

  /* Keyboard focus is drawn here rather than inherited from the browser default, which the
     base button rule above sits next to: it removes the border the default ring is drawn
     against. 3px solid #1d4ed8 is 6.7:1 against #fff and 6.2:1 against the #f5f5f5 body,
     both well past the 3:1 a non-text indicator needs. :focus-visible and not :focus — a
     ring that follows the mouse nags everyone who did not ask for one. */
  :focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #1d4ed8; outline-offset: 2px; }

No `border-radius` on that rule: the outline already follows the element's own radius, and setting it here would square off the group row while it is focused.
--- (2) STYLESHEET: change `padding: 12px` to `padding: 0` on the existing `.group-list li` rule (line 20), and add the three rules below immediately after the existing `.group-list li:hover` rule (line 21) ---

  /* The row is a real button now, so the whole card is its hit area: the padding moved off
     the li and onto the button rather than leaving a text-only target inside a padded card.
     Rendered size is unchanged. */
  .group-list button { display: block; width: 100%; padding: 12px; border: none; border-radius: 8px; background: none; color: inherit; font: inherit; text-align: left; }
  /* button:hover is also specificity 0,1,1 and is declared earlier in this file, so without
     this later rule the indigo hover fill sits on top of the card under the pointer. The
     card's own hover lives on .group-list li:hover and still applies. */
  .group-list button:hover { background: none; }
  /* The ring is drawn just outside the button, so the card behind it does not otherwise
     look touched by focus. */
  .group-list li:focus-within { background: #e5e7eb; }

Leave `cursor: pointer` on `.group-list li` alone — it is redundant now that the button supplies it, and keeping it changes nothing.
--- (3) STYLESHEET: replace the existing `.back-link` rule (line 37) with the two rules below, and fold the existing `.back-link:hover` rule (line 38) into them ---

  .back-link { background: none; border: none; padding: 0; color: #2563eb; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.9rem; margin-bottom: 12px; display: inline-block; text-align: left; }
  /* button:hover is 0,1,1 and outranks .back-link (0,1,0), so the hover fill has to be
     cancelled here explicitly or the back control becomes a solid indigo block on hover. */
  .back-link:hover { background: none; text-decoration: underline; }

The visible text of the control is unchanged: same blue, same underline on hover, same size and spacing. `display: inline-block` is kept so the control does not stretch to the width of the panel as a block-level button would.
--- (4) MARKUP: line 74, the back control. It STAYS at line 74, ahead of the heading ---

  -    <span class="back-link" id="back-link">← Back to groups</span>
  +    <button type="button" class="back-link" id="back-link">← Back to groups</button>

`type="button"` is explicit even though it sits outside any form. The class and the id both stay, so the existing CSS and the documented #back-link selector keep working. The `←` stays in the accessible name; "← Back to groups" reads correctly spoken aloud.
--- (5) MARKUP: line 75, the group heading ---

  -    <h1 id="group-title"></h1>
  +    <h1 id="group-title" tabindex="-1"></h1>

tabindex="-1" is what lets focus be moved onto the heading programmatically. It does NOT put the heading in the Tab order. Reading the group's tabbable sequence from the markup as it now stands: the back control, then the heading (skipped), then #expense-desc. So from the back control, Tab goes to #expense-desc; from #expense-desc, Shift+Tab goes back to the back control. AC-3 checks the second of those.
--- (6) SCRIPT: five changes inside the inline <script>, in this order ---

(6a) Immediately after the `let currentMembers = null;` declaration and its comment (line 159), add:

  // The group list is rebuilt on every load, so the row the user opened is gone by the
  // time they come back to it. Focus is restored by group id rather than by holding on
  // to a button that has been thrown away.
  let lastOpenedGroupId = null;

(6b) Replace the whole of showCreateView() (lines 169-175) with:

  function showCreateView() {
    document.getElementById('view-create-group').classList.remove('hidden');
    document.getElementById('view-group').classList.add('hidden');
    currentGroupId = null;
    currentMembers = null;
    // A heading belonging to a view that is no longer on screen. Left alone it keeps the
    // last group's name in the DOM, and the create view's own h1 is the one that should
    // be read out.
    document.getElementById('group-title').textContent = '';
    // Focus follows the user back to the row they opened. The list is rebuilt by the load
    // below, so the row has to be looked up after that load resolves, not now — until it
    // does, focus is on <body>.
    loadGroups()
      .then(() => {
        const rows = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('#group-list .group-item'));
        const target =
          rows.find((row) => row.dataset.groupId === lastOpenedGroupId) ||
          rows[0] ||
          document.getElementById('group-name');
        if (target) target.focus();
      })
      // The list's own error path is unchanged by this ticket, and a failed focus restore
      // is not worth an unhandled rejection. Matched by id rather than by building a
      // selector string out of it, so the id never reaches a query.
      .catch(() => {});
  }

The three-way fallback is deliberate, and it is defensive rather than a path the app can currently reach: a group opened from the create form has no row yet when the list next loads, and with no groups at all the last arm sends focus to #group-name, the first thing the user would do next. The arms are kept whole rather than trimmed, and AC-11 records that only a test can exercise them, because nothing in this app deletes a group.

(6c) In showGroupView() (lines 177-187): add `lastOpenedGroupId = groupId;` immediately after `currentGroupId = groupId;`, add the placeholder immediately before the split-mode line, and replace the bare `loadGroup(groupId);` on the last line. The function becomes:

  function showGroupView(groupId) {
    document.getElementById('view-create-group').classList.add('hidden');
    document.getElementById('view-group').classList.remove('hidden');
    currentGroupId = groupId;
    lastOpenedGroupId = groupId;
    // The heading carries no name until the fetch resolves, which leaves the view untitled
    // for as long as the request takes. This is a name, not a spinner: it is the same h1
    // that is focused below, so the view is never on screen with an empty heading.
    document.getElementById('group-title').textContent = 'Loading…';
    // Opening a group starts on equal split: the mode is a choice about the expense being
    // typed now, and carrying 'by shares' from the last group into a fresh one would make
    // the default depend on where the user had been.
    document.querySelector('input[name="split-mode"][value="equally"]').checked = true;
    syncShareInputs();
    // Focus lands on the heading only once the real name is in it. Focusing first would
    // read out the placeholder and leave the user on the wrong title. Because the heading
    // is tabindex="-1", pressing Tab from here moves forward past it to the description
    // field — the back control is the stop before that, reached with Shift+Tab (AC-3).
    loadGroup(groupId)
      .then(() => {
        document.getElementById('group-title').focus();
      })
      // Same reason as in showCreateView: a load that fails is already reported by the
      // panels below the form, and it must not become an unhandled rejection here. The
      // heading keeps its placeholder — this ticket does not build the group view's
      // error state, which is out of scope.
      .catch(() => {});
  }

(6d) In loadGroups() (lines 197-202), replace the body of the `for (const group of data.groups)` loop with:

    const li = document.createElement('li');
    // A button rather than a listener on the li: this list is how the whole app is
    // navigated, and an element that only answers a click cannot be reached by Tab or
    // announced as something to activate. The li stays, so the list is still a list of
    // rows, and the name still goes in as text for the reason given in renderBalances.
    const button = document.createElement('button');
    button.type = 'button';
    button.className = 'group-item';
    button.dataset.groupId = group.id;
    button.textContent = group.name;
    button.addEventListener('click', () => showGroupView(group.id));
    li.appendChild(button);
    list.appendChild(li);

The empty-state `list.innerHTML = '<li class="empty-state">No groups yet. Create one above.</li>'` above the loop is NOT a control and is left exactly as it is. The row's accessible name is the group name, carried by its text content — no aria-label is needed or wanted, because the visible label is already the name.

(6e) Immediately above the existing `// Back link` comment and its listener (lines 569-570), insert the Escape handler:

  // Escape is the second way back, and it works from wherever focus is — including from
  // inside the description or amount field, because a half-typed expense is not a reason
  // to trap someone in this view. On the create-group view it does nothing: there is
  // nothing there to go back to.
  document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
    if (e.key !== 'Escape') return;
    if (document.getElementById('view-group').classList.contains('hidden')) return;
    e.preventDefault();
    showCreateView();
  });

The existing line `document.getElementById('back-link').addEventListener('click', showCreateView);` is UNCHANGED and needs no key handler of its own: a <button> dispatches click on both Enter and Space natively, which is the whole point of (4).
